CoMD, link-cell molecular dynamics, distributed decomposition.

Same physics and same problem instance as the sibling port in ../sdsc: an FCC
copper lattice integrated with velocity Verlet under a Lennard-Jones potential,
one datablock per link cell, and the same "Final energy" validation scalar.
What differs is who builds the work and how often a cell is touched:

  - a step is ONE task per cell: it takes in the atoms that drifted into the
    cell, computes the forces against every atom within reach, kicks, takes
    the energy point when the block ends, kicks again and drifts.  Each cell
    keeps two generations of its block; a step reads one generation of its
    own cell and of its 26 neighbours and writes the other, so no block has a
    reader and a writer in the same step and a neighbour's block crosses the
    fabric once per step;
  - atoms move with the same read: a block holds its atoms where the drift
    left them, and every reader places each atom by its position, so the
    ones now inside the reader's cell are its arrivals and the rest are
    neighbour atoms under their cell's periodic shift;
  - a neighbour in another brick is read from that brick's halo, never from
    the cell itself: each brick packs the boundary layer facing a neighbour
    into runs of at most HALO_CHUNK cells once per step, one task on the
    reading brick pulls each run once, and the cell tasks take their
    neighbours out of the run they land in -- so what a boundary costs the
    fabric is a fixed number of messages per run per step, never one per
    cell.  A step is therefore two phases where there is more than one rank:
    the halo of the generation it reads, then its cell tasks;
  - every phase's fan-out is a spawn tree, so the tasks of a phase are emitted
    by as many tasks as the machine has room for instead of by one loop;
  - every phase's fan-in is a reduction tree, so no task ever holds a
    dependence per cell; the step's tree carries both energies and the atom
    count;
  - cells are generated in place by the tasks that will own them, from a
    generator that is a pure function of the atom's global index;
  - cells are blocked over a three-dimensional rank grid rather than laid out
    in index order, and every tree fans over the ranks before it fans over a
    rank's brick, so a leaf's cells share one owner and the leaf, its tasks
    and its collector all sit on that rank.

  -w/--width  link cells emitted by one spawn-tree leaf (default 8); a leaf
              never crosses a brick, so the spawn frontier is the sum over
              ranks of ceil(brick cells / width).

Build with the parent project; sources: atoms.c cells.c comd.c command.c fan.c
lj.c simulation.c timers.c timestep.c.
